61 17232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116135 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7cf9141d-1c90-5675-a333-b0aa4e0f3cef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116135 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116136 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116136 #edb/ref 1061 17233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116136 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e2dcd832-c069-5309-a7a3-27850adf28e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116136 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116498 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find the inverse of the matrix \n\n![](images/fd/fd9e9dc44e27810ef490a898a7c82ae9296eaf85ac9406cf310afe86638f71e1.svg) \n\nWe can compute the inverse of $A$ using elementary row operations. There are two key advantages to using row operations to compute the inverse of a matrix:\n\n- The method works for any invertible matrix of any size, not just $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\n- It requires significantly less computation than some alternative methods. This is especially true for large matrices.\n\nHere, we'll focus on the $2\\times 2$ case. We use the following fact:\n\n*The row operations that transform $A$ into the identity matrix $I_2$ also transform $I_2$ into $A^{-1}$*.\n\nSo, to find the inverse of $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}A$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/e5/e553bcc265698432452c8d35b79fe9dc70c25010a0bcc6874ad0302ea7033ed7.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,{I}_{2}\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\,I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n![](images/b7/b7c08e9a28ebfe456a9d77ccdaf6688893dfd04ba755a989a417c8a43e80ee0c.svg)\n\nWe can easily check our result by computing $AA^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116498 #edb/ref 1065 3167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116498 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fc38ae42-2cd4-53df-9ad5-568d8e3b5fd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116498 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186118497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186118498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186118499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186118500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186118501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118496 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118496 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118496 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186118500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118496 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "82d94d35-2966-563a-ae36-53ee87337d99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118497 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118497 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118497 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "826165fc-f641-5f1a-94bd-36d5b21a3d8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118498 #edb/ref 1080 "-11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118498 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118498 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b7e517cb-5c7c-5901-9fc8-4d62de0ffa87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186118499 #edb/r